WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.294
Apple Silicon systems combine CPU cores, GPU cores, unified memory, storage, and specialized accelerators inside an integrated platform.

00:00:09.294 --> 00:00:17.016
Performance cores handle demanding CPU work, while efficiency cores help with lighter tasks and power management.

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:25.353
GPU cores can accelerate supported local inference workflows through runtimes that use Apple's graphics and compute stack.

00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:30.957
Storage speed affects model loading, dataset handling, and general responsiveness.

00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:41.071
The Neural Engine is important in Apple's broader machine learning ecosystem, but local LLM runtimes do not automatically use it for every workload.

00:00:41.071 --> 00:00:49.818
Many local language model workflows rely more heavily on CPU and GPU execution paths, especially through Metal-enabled runtimes.

00:00:49.818 --> 00:00:58.497
It is useful to know the Neural Engine exists, but it should not be assumed to solve every local inference performance problem.

00:00:58.497 --> 00:01:02.187
Unified memory is one of the biggest planning factors.

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:11.276
A Mac with more memory can often run larger models, larger context windows, or more simultaneous services than a lower-memory system.

00:01:11.276 --> 00:01:20.775
But total memory is shared by macOS, applications, model weights, runtime overhead, context buffers, browser tabs, and background services.

00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:26.583
Available memory for inference is always less than the headline system memory number.

00:01:26.583 --> 00:01:31.709
Thermal design, battery, and power behavior matter for sustained workloads.

00:01:31.709 --> 00:01:38.474
A MacBook may run a short test quickly but reduce performance during long generation or batch work.

00:01:38.474 --> 00:01:41.754
A desktop Mac may sustain load more comfortably.

00:01:41.754 --> 00:01:54.533
When planning an Apple Silicon LLM setup, think about memory capacity, memory bandwidth, cooling, storage space, and whether the machine will be used interactively, as a service, or both.
